os(\\theta)" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299500 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534624 true] [17592186299500 1111 #uuid "d62cf37c-d928-5471-a730-29da52a950e2" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299501 1000 #edb/ref 17592186299502 13194139534624 true] [17592186299501 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534624 true] [17592186299501 1041 "field-2" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299501 1104 #edb/ref 17592186299502 13194139534624 true] [17592186299501 1110 #uuid "54e65f65-c0b2-5b94-8cf9-7800c70d0e79" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299502 1034 "10\\sin(\\theta)" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299502 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534624 true] [17592186299502 1111 #uuid "4412556e-6fb8-579f-ae78-4929da26e1aa" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299503 1017 #edb/ref 17592186299504 13194139534624 true] [17592186299503 1017 #edb/ref 17592186299506 13194139534624 true] [17592186299503 1089 "q-321947" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299503 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534624 true] [17592186299503 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/11f1e6651d86e0b00884091338029395cd6d2ff44f25b97d010ec750d13bef59.png)\n\nThe arc of a circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299503 1113 #uuid "bf86fc72-2a3b-5b81-8f85-40f278af2f6f" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299503 1145 "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=4.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/2a281b909e4a3577ea3774e9d75c43d97e4d4a69d0f673eed4189834e1ed1c43.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}].\n$$" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299504 1000 #edb/ref 17592186299505 13194139534624 true] [17592186299504 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534624 true] [17592186299504 1041 "field-1" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299504 1104 #edb/ref 17592186299505 13194139534624 true] [17592186299504 1110 #uuid "0e29c233-ffb8-54e7-aaa0-7c423e7a89d1" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299505 1034 "0" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299505 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534624 true] [17592186299505 1111 #uuid "eaaabe4b-630b-5cf4-b94b-265158077688" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299506 1000 #edb/ref 17592186299507 13194139534624 true] [17592186299506 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534624 true] [17592186299506 1041 "field-2" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299506 1104 #edb/ref 17592186299507 13194139534624 true] [17592186299506 1110 #uuid "35443237-cdfc-5e40-a53d-b48d7ae809aa" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299507 1034 "\\frac{7\\pi}{6}" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299507 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534624 true] [17592186299507 1111 #uuid "67fab322-e6f6-592d-924d-ebe7be333936" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299508 1017 #edb/ref 17592186299509 13194139534624 true] [17592186299508 1017 #edb/ref 17592186299511 13194139534624 true] [17592186299508 1017 #edb/ref 17592186299513 13194139534624 true] [17592186299508 1089 "q-321986" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299508 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534624 true] [17592186299508 1100 "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +4,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}+(y\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-3}}." 13194139534624 true] [17592186299508 1113 #uuid "4ee65cb6-ef5d-5634-9e99-c2bd8cbc9a51" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299508 1145 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+3}{5} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-4}{5} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{25} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is $(x+3)^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=25.$" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299509 1000 #edb/ref 17592186299510 13194139534624 true] [17592186299509 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534624 true] [17592186299509 1041 "field-1" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299509 1104 #edb/ref 17592186299510 13194139534624 true] [17592186299509 1110 #uuid "5944889b-3977-56df-bc2a-70ba1591264d" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299510 1034 "3" 13194139534624 tru