\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=3.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a three-quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/6f27674dd7382f9040cdeb8e85ea35ff63e581ef5752b373aa0ab87a0b3e2f0f.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]$, we get the required three-quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the three-quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299544 1000 #edb/ref 17592186299545 13194139534624 true] [17592186299544 1000 #edb/ref 17592186299546 13194139534624 true] [17592186299544 1000 #edb/ref 17592186299547 13194139534624 true] [17592186299544 1000 #edb/ref 17592186299548 13194139534624 true] [17592186299544 1000 #edb/ref 17592186299549 13194139534624 true] [17592186299544 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534624 true] [17592186299544 1041 "selection" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299544 1104 #edb/ref 17592186299548 13194139534624 true] [17592186299544 1110 #uuid "978b3bf0-a398-5c0c-a0df-b249d600a836" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299545 1034 "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299545 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534624 true] [17592186299545 1111 #uuid "b2a75d7f-a83a-53d2-91cf-19783575a28b" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299546 1034 "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}]" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299546 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534624 true] [17592186299546 1111 #uuid "f25996bf-fd27-5e29-89e3-7d488619155e" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299547 1034 "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299547 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534624 true] [17592186299547 1111 #uuid "22c3653e-9278-5059-86c9-30d610217067" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299548 1034 "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299548 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534624 true] [17592186299548 1111 #uuid "fc478a22-4762-53c5-90b4-bcbae6233313" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299549 1034 "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}]" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299549 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534624 true] [17592186299549 1111 #uuid "e655fb51-22af-560b-911f-fc4ad0e14612" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299550 1017 #edb/ref 17592186299551 13194139534624 true] [17592186299550 1089 "q-79168" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299550 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534624 true] [17592186299550 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/a0ec11769fa20feb317ef502f41e1d31ffe539d4b17a51ad46b8487c17752021.png)\n\nThe arc of a circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299550 1113 #uuid "97130ad5-e5a5-5a74-b63b-c6293a7f474e" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299550 1145 "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=2.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/9752e135eee9c4e585a007f84b0b8b8d220d4e2e1f027fc8a69aebf27066940e.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}].\n$$" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299551 1000 #edb/ref 17592186299552 13194139534624 true] [17592186299551 1000 #edb/ref 17592186299553 13194139534624 true] [17592186299551 1000 #edb/ref 17592186299554 13194139534624 true] [17592186299551 1000 #edb/ref 17592186299555 13194139534624 true] [17592186299551 1000 #edb/ref 17592186299556 13194139534624 true] [17592186299551 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534624 true] [17592186299551 1041 "selection" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299551 1104 #edb/ref 17592186299552 13194139534624 true] [17592186299551 1110 #uuid "e0f3f8b1-a855-5cac-8697-2e89fcb5b44f" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299552 1034 "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}]" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299552 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534624 true] [17592186299552 1111 #uuid "65dbc84a-947b-5650-8306-f0dd3bd145cc" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299553 1034 "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}]" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299553 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534624 true] [17592186299553 1111 #uuid "32676c82-5ca7-56cc-94f5-d84d3d35c7bd" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299554 1034 "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{5\\pi }{2}]" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299554 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534624 true] [17592186299554 1111 #uuid "a678dc53-3d82-5faf-a5ce-08ade66383a8" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299555 1034 "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299555 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534624 true] [17592186299555 1111 #uuid "16d4ea9e-0021-5012-8ae3-12b0428a6559" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299556 1034 "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}]" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299556 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534624 true] [17592186299556 1111 #uuid "f7336668-a6bc-5de5-8669-8be21fc89fa9" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299557 1017 #edb/ref 17592186299558 13194139534624 true] [17592186299557 1089 "q-79222" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299557 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534624 true] [17592186299557 1100 "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299557 1113 #uuid "d6f537a3-5f7e-5f24-bef9-d798370c0a41" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299557 1145 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-5}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{4} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ (x-5)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $(x-5)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$" 13194139534624 true] [17592186299558 1000 #edb/ref 17592186299559 13194139534624 true] [17592186299558 1000 #edb/ref 17592186299560 13194139534624 true] [17592186299558 1000 #edb/ref 17592186299561 13194139534624 true] [17592186